Reset the SEP coprocessor CPU before sending MSG_BOOT_TZ0.

Touch ID reverse-engineering (~/Projects/MacFingerprint, findings/phase2d-...md).
Kernel: AsahiLinux/linux asahi-7.1.5-2 (drivers/soc/apple/sep.rs). Applied LAST, after
the other touchid sep.rs patches (context regenerated against that stacked state).

Problem: iBoot boots the SEP (SEPFW running) before Linux gets control. The apple_sep
stub then sends TZ0 to a SEP whose SEPROM is no longer listening -> the SEP stays silent
(no TZ0/getrandom reply). Keeping the power domain on (pd_ignore_unused) does not help:
it is a coprocessor-state problem, not a power problem.

Fix (from macOS AppleA7IOP-ASCWrap-v4 _runCPU/stopCPU, decompiled): restart the SEP CPU
via the ASC CPU_CONTROL register before TZ0. It lives in the sep node's own reg0 range
(sep@396400000, "reg = <0x3 0x96400000 0x0 0x6C000>") at offset 0x44, bit4 (0x10) = RUN.
This is AP-accessible ASC MMIO -- NOT the AP-locked PMGR reset register that crashed
earlier. stopCPU = clear bits 4+5, runCPU = set bit4 -> SEP re-enters SEPROM and accepts
the TZ0/IMG4/SET_SHMEM handshake the driver already sends.

Note: reg0 (0x396400000, 0x6c000) overlaps the sep_mbox range (0x396408000); we use the
non-exclusive iomap() (plain ioremap, no request_mem_region) so it does not conflict with
the apple-mailbox driver. The one-shot mapping is released right after the reset.
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/sep.rs
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/sep.rs
@@ -371,6 +371,32 @@
             },
         )?;
         *data.mbox.lock() = Some(Mailbox::new_byname(pdev.as_ref(), c"mbox", data.clone())?);
+        // MacFingerprint (Touch ID RE): reset the SEP coprocessor CPU before TZ0.
+        // iBoot already booted the SEP (SEPFW running) -> its SEPROM ignores the
+        // TZ0 below and the SEP stays silent. macOS (AppleA7IOP-ASCWrap-v4
+        // _runCPU/stopCPU) restarts the CPU via ASC CPU_CONTROL at reg0
+        // (sep@396400000) + 0x44, bit4 (0x10) = RUN. AP-accessible ASC MMIO, NOT
+        // the AP-locked PMGR reset register. (Diagnostic: read back the register.)
+        {
+            use kernel::io::Io; // try_read32/try_write32 live on the Io trait
+            const ASC_CPU_CONTROL: usize = 0x44;
+            let request = pdev.io_request_by_index(0).ok_or(EIO)?;
+            let iomem = request.iomap();
+            let iomem = KBox::pin_init(iomem, GFP_KERNEL)?;
+            let io = iomem.access(pdev.as_ref())?;
+            let before = io.try_read32(ASC_CPU_CONTROL)?;
+            io.try_write32(before & !0x30, ASC_CPU_CONTROL)?; // stopCPU: clear RUN(bit4)+bit5
+            let stopped = io.try_read32(ASC_CPU_CONTROL)?;
+            io.try_write32((before & !0x30) | 0x10, ASC_CPU_CONTROL)?; // runCPU: set RUN(bit4)
+            let running = io.try_read32(ASC_CPU_CONTROL)?;
+            dev_info!(
+                pdev.as_ref(),
+                "SEP: CPU_CONTROL+0x44 before={:#x} stopped={:#x} running={:#x} (reset before TZ0)",
+                before,
+                stopped,
+                running
+            );
+        }
         data.start()?;
         Ok(Self(data))
     }
